('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,5,'Lena was always afraid of the dark. From childhood, she felt shadows moving and whispering to her. But tonight was different. She wanted to be brave. Taking a deep breath, she switched off the lights and stood in the center of her room. Her heartbeat as she attempted to get rid of the fear building up on her. For a while, there was nothing. No sound. And then, after her eyes were adjusted, Lena saw the thin light of moonlight coming from the window. The shadows no longer frightened her. She laughed at herself thinking that possibly the dark didn''t have to be as dreadful as she''d imagined.','How did Lena feel at the end of the passage?','Angry','Amused','Sleepy','Afraid','B','She laughed at herself, realizing the dark wasn''t as dreadful as she thought.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,6,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','What is the main topic of the poem?','Trees','Gardening','Words','Music','C','The poem focuses on the power and impact of words.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,7,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','What do words help to plant, according to the poem?','Fruits','Trees','Flowers','Dreams','B','"With each thought, we plant a tree" is a metaphor used in the poem.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,8,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','What grows in a sentence?','Joy','Rain','Truth','Fear','C','The line "In each sentence, truth can grow" says this directly.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,9,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','What can a seed represent in the poem?','A toy','A lie','Knowledge','A sound','C','"A seed of knowledge we can sow" shows this symbolic meaning.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,10,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','What is the poet encouraging readers to do with words?','Use them carelessly','Stay silent','Use them to create and share truth','Write only','C','The poet is encouraging readers to see the power of words — not to use them randomly or stay silent, but to use words thoughtfully to express truth, meaning, and connection. This shows the value of communication that builds understanding and shares what is real or meaningful.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,11,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','Who is the quiet one in the group?','Emily','Sarah','Lisa','Anna','B','The passage begins by describing Sarah as the quiet one.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,12,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','Why did Emily come to Sarah?','To borrow a book','To play a game','To talk about her troubles','To give her a gift','C','Emily is upset and wants to share her feelings.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,13,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','What did Sarah mostly do during their conversation?','Gave advice','Laughed','Listened silently','Wrote everything down','C','Sarah listened without interrupting, only nodding or smiling',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,14,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','What was Emily’s reaction after talking to Sarah?','She left angrily','She cried harder','She felt calmer','She fell asleep','C','Emily expressed gratitude and seemed comforted.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,15,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','What is described as the “greatest gift” in the story?','A hug','Speaking wisely','Listening and being present','Writing a letter','C','The story concludes by stating this message directly.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,1,16,'"The Impact of Social Media Addiction"\n\nMany teenagers spend excessive hours on social media, often neglecting schoolwork and sleep. To combat this issue, schools have implemented digital wellness programs. Parents are also using screen time limits and encouraging offline activities. Additionally, app developers are creating tools that help users track and limit their social media usage. Constant social media triggers dopamine releases in the brain, creating addictive behavior patterns. This addiction leads to decreased concentration in class and lower academic performance. Because students are constantly checking their phones, they experience disrupted sleep patterns. As a result, many teenagers report feeling anxious and exhausted. While previous generations spent their free time in face-to-face interactions, today''s youth primarily connect through screens. Traditional social gatherings involve physical activities and direct communication, but modern social life often consists of likes, shares, and virtual conversations. However, both forms of interaction satisfy the basic human need for connection, albeit in different ways.','What age group is mainly affected by social media addiction in the passage?','Adults','Children','Teenagers','Seniors','C','The passage specifically states that "many teenagers" are affected.',NULL,0,1),
