to questions.','What concern does Alex raise about the cost of uniforms?','Uniforms are too cheap to be durable','They often go out of style quickly','They can be an extra burden for families','Uniforms are only sold at expensive stores','C','Alex argues that for some families, buying uniforms can be a financial burden.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,32,'Moderator: Welcome to today''s debate! The topic is whether school uniforms should be mandatory in schools. Let’s begin with the opening statements. Sarah, you have the floor. Sarah (Pro-School Uniforms): Thank you. I believe that school uniforms should be mandatory because they promote equality among students. When everyone wears the same outfit, there is less pressure to compete based on clothing, which can reduce bullying and distractions. Uniforms also help students focus more on their studies instead of worrying about fashion. Moderator: Thank you, Sarah. Alex, your opening statement? Alex (Against School Uniforms): I disagree with Sarah. While uniforms might reduce some pressure, they also take away students'' freedom to express themselves. Clothing is a way for students to show their personality and creativity. By forcing everyone to wear the same thing, schools are limiting individual expression. Plus, uniforms can be uncomfortable and expensive for some families. Moderator: Thank you, Alex. Sarah, would you like to respond? Sarah (Pro-School Uniforms): Yes, I understand that some students may feel that uniforms limit their freedom, but the point of school is to learn, not to focus on what you''re wearing. Uniforms can actually make mornings easier because students don''t have to decide what to wear. Additionally, schools should be places where everyone is treated equally, regardless of how much money their families have. Moderator: Alex, your response? Alex (Against School Uniforms): While I agree that equality is important, forcing students to wear uniforms doesn''t really solve the issue. Instead of focusing on clothes, we should teach students to appreciate differences and respect others'' choices. Also, if families can''t afford uniforms, that creates an extra burden on them. Schools should focus on making education better, not controlling what students wear. Moderator: Thank you both for your arguments. We’ll now open the floor to questions.','According to Alex, what should schools focus on instead of controlling clothing?','Improving cafeteria food','Teaching respect and appreciation for differences','Organizing fashion shows','Extending recess time','B','Alex believes schools should focus on teaching students to respect and appreciate differences rather than enforcing uniform policies.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,33,NULL,'Which of the following statements is an example of empirical evidence?','A study found that people who exercise regularly live longer.','Chocolate ice cream is the best flavor.','I believe electric cars are the future because they reduce carbon emissions.','A triangle has three sides.','A','Empirical evidence is based on data, research, or observations that have been systematically collected. Option A refers to a study, which implies it is based on empirical data. The other options are opinions or facts.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,34,NULL,'Which statement is an example of self-evidence?','If you drop something, it will fall due to gravity.','The climate scientist stated that global warming is supported by evidence.','I think social media is harmful to mental health.','My friend lost 20 pounds by cutting out sugar.','A','Self-evidence refers to something that is true by definition or universally accepted without the need for additional proof. In this case, the statement about gravity is universally accepted and doesn’t require further evidence to be understood as true.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,35,NULL,'Which of the following statements is an example of an argument from authority?','The capital of France is Paris.','A doctor recommended this medication because it’s the best treatment for my condition.','I think city life is more exciting than rural life.','Research shows that people who sleep for 7-8 hours per night are healthier on average.','B','An argument from authority relies on the expertise or credibility of a person or source to support the claim. Option B refers to the advice given by a doctor, leveraging their authority in the medical field.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,36,NULL,'Which of the following is an example of a statement of fact?','The capital of France is Paris.','I think social media is harmful to mental health.','Chocolate ice cream is the best flavor.','My friend lost 20 pounds by cutting out sugar, so I think it''s the best way to lose weight.','A','This is a statement of fact because it is objectively true and can be verified. The other options are opinions or personal experiences.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,37,NULL,'Which statement below best represents an opinion rather than a fact?','All bachelors are unmarried men.','Social media apps are used by millions of people every day.','Water boils at 100°C under standard atmospheric pressure.','Given the rapid advancements in AI, I believe it will soon be integrated into every industry.','D','Option D is a statement of opinion because it includes a personal belief or prediction, not a verifiable fact.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,38,NULL,'Which of the following is an example of anecdotal evidence?','A study found that plants grow faster when exposed to more sunlight.','My friend lost 20 pounds by cutting out sugar, so I think it''s the best way to lose weight.','According to Einstein''s theory of relativity, time slows down as you approach the speed of light.','A survey of 1,000 people found that 80% of them prefer online shopping over traditional retail.','B','This is an anecdotal account based on an individual''s personal experience rather than statistical or empirical evidence.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,39,NULL,'Which of the following statements is an example of empirical evidence?','Research shows that people who sleep for 7-8 hours per night are healthier on average.','I believe electric cars are the future, considering how much cleaner they are compared to gasoline-powered cars.','All bachelors are unmarried men.','The climate scientist stated that the evidence overwhelmingly supports the reality of global warming.','A','Empirical evidence is based on observable data or research. The other options involve opinions, statements from authority, or self-evidence.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,40,NULL,'Which of the following is an example of an argument from authority?','A triangle has three sides.','The doctor recommended this medication because it is the most effective treatment for my condition.','My friend felt a lot more energetic when she tried a new diet, so I think it''s the best way to improve health.','Given the rapid advancements in AI, I believe it will soon be integrated into every industry.','B','This is an argument from authority because it relies on the expertise of the doctor. The other options involve self-evidence, opinions, or logical claims.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,41,'"Maria Cristina Falls – The Power and Pride of the Nation!" "Nestled in the heart of Mindanao, Maria Cristina Falls is not just a waterfall—it''s the beating heart of Philippine power and pride. Known as the ''Mother of Industry'' and the lifeblood of Iligan City, this majestic wonder fuels the economy, inspires the people, and showcases the unrivaled beauty of our land. If you haven’t witnessed its roaring grace, are you even truly Filipino? Tourists worldwide flock to see what we’re lucky to call ours. Don''t be the last to experience the pride of the nation—visit Maria Cristina Falls today!"','What propaganda technique is used in the phrase “Don’t be the last to experience the pride of the nation”?','Testimonial','Glittering Generalities','Bandwagon','Name Calling','C','This line pressures the reader to join others in visiting the falls, a classic example of the Bandwagon technique.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,42,NULL,'Which technique involves using vague, emotionally appealing words without facts?','Plain Folks','Glittering Generalities','Transfer','Card Stacking','B','Words like "majestic" and "unrivaled beauty" make the message sound appealing without supporting evidence.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,43,NULL,'Which propaganda technique is used in the line: "If you haven’t witnessed its roaring grace, are you even truly Filipino?"','Testimonial','Bandwagon','Fear','Name-calling','B','This line suggests that all true Filipinos have seen the falls, pressuring others to conform and not be left out, which is characteristic of the bandwagon technique.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,44,NULL,'What propaganda technique is being used when the falls are described as "the beating heart of Philippine power and pride"?','Glittering Generalities','Testimonial','Plain Folks','Card Stacking','A','The phrase uses emotionally appealing, vague but positive terms like “power” and “pride” to stir patriotic feelings without providing concrete evidence— hallmarks of glittering generalities.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,45,NULL,'Which propaganda technique is demonstrated by referring to Maria Cristina Falls as "the Mother of Industry"?','Name-calling','Transfer','Glittering Generalities','Testimonial','C','The phrase "the Mother of Industry" uses emotional and positive-sounding words to create a favorable impression of Maria Cristina Falls. This is an example of Glittering Generalities, a technique that uses vague, emotionally appealing language to inspire approval without presenting concrete evidence.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,46,NULL,'What propaganda technique is used in the phrase: “Don’t be the last to experience the pride of the nation?”','Fear','Testimonial','Bandwagon','Transfer','C','This line pressures the reader to act quickly by implying that others are already participating, and you shouldn''t be left behind—typical of bandwagon.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,47,NULL,'Which technique is implied in the statement: "Tourists worldwide flock to see what we’re lucky to call ours"?','Testimonial','Fear','Bandwagon','Transfer','A','The use of tourists'' admiration acts as a form of indirect testimonial, suggesting that the falls must be special if people from around the world come to see them.',NULL,0,0),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,48,NULL,'“If you haven’t witnessed its roaring grace, are you even truly Filipino?” is an example of:','Fear','Testimonial','Appeal to Nationalism','Bandwagon','C','This line appeals to the reader''s sense of national pride and identity, urging them to act to prove patriotism.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,49,'LEARN ANYWHERE, SUCCEED EVERYWHERE! ! & % $ # "\n\nAlternative Learning System (ALS): Your Future, Your Way!\n\nWhat is ALS? The Alternative Learning System (ALS) is a flexible learning program by the Department of Education for out-of-school youth, adults, and other marginalized learners who want to finish basic education outside the formal school system.'' ) "You don’t need a classroom to ( change your life—just courage and a choice." . Why Choose ALS? - , + * Flexible Schedule – Learn at your own pace. Accreditation & Certification – Earn a certificate equivalent to elementary or high school diploma. Skills-Based Curriculum – Real-life skills for real-world success. Second Chances for All – Everyone deserves to learn—no matter the age, background, or status.','What propaganda technique is used in the slogan “Join the thousands who chose ALS to change their future!”?','Testimonial','Bandwagon','Name-calling','Card stacking','B','Bandwagon encourages people to join because “everyone else is doing it.” It appeals to the desire to fit in or not be left behind.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,2,50,'LEARN ANYWHERE, SUCCEED EVERYWHERE! ! & % $ # "\n\nAlternative Learning System (ALS): Your Future, Your Way!\n\nWhat is ALS? The Alternative Learning System (ALS) is a flexible learning program by the Department of Education for out-of-school youth, adults, and other marginalized learners who want to finish basic education outside the formal school system.'' ) "You don’t need a classroom to ( change your life—just courage and a choice." . Why Choose ALS? - , + * Flexible Schedule – Learn at your own pace. Accreditation & Certification – Earn a certificate equivalent to elementary or high school diploma. Skills-Based Curriculum – Real-life skills for real-world success. Second Chances for All – Everyone deserves to learn—no matter the age, background, or status.','Which line from the ALS pamphlet uses a "Glittering Generality" to emotionally appeal to the reader without giving specific details?','“Learn at your own pace.”','“Real-life skills for real-world success.”','“Earn a certificate equivalent to a high school diploma.”','“You don’t need a classroom to change your life—just courage and a choice.”','D','This line is an example of a Glittering Generality because it uses emotional and inspiring words like "change your life," "courage," and "choice" without providing concrete facts or specific details. These positive-sounding terms are designed to motivate and uplift, but they are vague and open to interpretation.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,51,'The Water Cycle The water cycle is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. It involves several key processes, including evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. Evaporation occurs when the sun heats up water in rivers, lakes, or oceans, causing it to turn into water vapor and rise into the air. Condensation happens when the water vapor cools and changes back into liquid droplets, forming clouds. When the clouds become heavy, the water falls back to the Earth as precipitation in the form of rain, snow, or hail. The water then collects in bodies of water or seeps into the ground, completing the cycle.','What is the process called when water turns into vapor and rises into the air?','Precipitation','Condensation','Evaporation','Collection','C','Evaporation is the process where water changes from liquid to vapor due to heat from the sun.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,52,'The Water Cycle The water cycle is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. It involves several key processes, including evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. Evaporation occurs when the sun heats up water in rivers, lakes, or oceans, causing it to turn into water vapor and rise into the air. Condensation happens when the water vapor cools and changes back into liquid droplets, forming clouds. When the clouds become heavy, the water falls back to the Earth as precipitation in the form of rain, snow, or hail. The water then collects in bodies of water or seeps into the ground, completing the cycle.','Which process causes water vapor to form clouds?','Condensation','Evaporation','Precipitation','Collection','A','Condensation is when water vapor cools down and becomes liquid droplets, forming clouds.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,53,'The Water Cycle The water cycle is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. It involves several key processes, including evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. Evaporation occurs when the sun heats up water in rivers, lakes, or oceans, causing it to turn into water vapor and rise into the air. Condensation happens when the water vapor cools and changes back into liquid droplets, forming clouds. When the clouds become heavy, the water falls back to the Earth as precipitation in the form of rain, snow, or hail. The water then collects in bodies of water or seeps into the ground, completing the cycle.','What form can precipitation take when it falls to the Earth?','Water vapor','Ice','Rain, snow, or hail','Fog','C','The text states that precipitation can occur as rain, snow, or hail when the clouds become heavy.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,54,'The Water Cycle The water cycle is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. It involves several key processes, including evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. Evaporation occurs when the sun heats up water in rivers, lakes, or oceans, causing it to turn into water vapor and rise into the air. Condensation happens when the water vapor cools and changes back into liquid droplets, forming clouds. When the clouds become heavy, the water falls back to the Earth as precipitation in the form of rain, snow, or hail. The water then collects in bodies of water or seeps into the ground, completing the cycle.','Who is the most likely target audience for this text?','Scientists studying climate change','Young students learning about nature','Farmers planning crop irrigation','Engineers designing water systems','B','The text uses simple vocabulary and clear explanations suitable for beginners, making it appropriate for young learners studying basic science.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,55,'The Water Cycle The water cycle is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. It involves several key processes, including evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. Evaporation occurs when the sun heats up water in rivers, lakes, or oceans, causing it to turn into water vapor and rise into the air. Condensation happens when the water vapor cools and changes back into liquid droplets, forming clouds. When the clouds become heavy, the water falls back to the Earth as precipitation in the form of rain, snow, or hail. The water then collects in bodies of water or seeps into the ground, completing the cycle.','What is the author''s main argument in this text?','Water is becoming scarce due to human activity','The water cycle causes global weather patterns','The water cycle is a continuous natural process','Rainfall patterns vary in different countries','C','The entire text explains how the water cycle works and emphasizes that it repeats continuously, making this the author’s main point.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,56,'Introduction\n\nDefinition: Linear texts are those that develop smoothly and logically from one stage to the next. They are typically read from beginning to end in a sequential manner. Examples include novels, essays, and letters. Non-Linear Texts Definition: Non-linear texts do not progress in a logical or sequential manner. They can be accessed from any point and are often used to present information visually. Examples include diagrams, timelines, charts, and maps. Importance of Understanding Linear and Non-Linear Texts Understanding these text types helps students interpret information more effectively. Non- linear texts, such as diagrams and charts, are particularly useful for visual learners and can convey complex information in a concise manner. Timeline of Development Grade 7: Learners begin to identify and distinguish between linear and non-linear texts. They learn to recognize examples of each type and understand their uses. Grade 9: Learners delve deeper into interpreting non-linear texts like diagrams and maps. They also learn to share opinions on how these texts convey ideas. Grade 10: Learners further explore linear and non-linear texts, focusing on their applications in real-world contexts.','A student is tasked with creating a presentation about the water cycle. Which type of text would be most effective for illustrating the process, and why?','Linear text (e.g., essay)','Non-linear text (e.g., diagram)','Both equally effective','Neither is effective','B','A diagram is more effective because it visually represents the cyclical process, allowing viewers to easily understand the relationships between different stages.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,57,'Introduction\n\nDefinition: Linear texts are those that develop smoothly and logically from one stage to the next. They are typically read from beginning to end in a sequential manner. Examples include novels, essays, and letters. Non-Linear Texts Definition: Non-linear texts do not progress in a logical or sequential manner. They can be accessed from any point and are often used to present information visually. Examples include diagrams, timelines, charts, and maps. Importance of Understanding Linear and Non-Linear Texts Understanding these text types helps students interpret information more effectively. Non- linear texts, such as diagrams and charts, are particularly useful for visual learners and can convey complex information in a concise manner. Timeline of Development Grade 7: Learners begin to identify and distinguish between linear and non-linear texts. They learn to recognize examples of each type and understand their uses. Grade 9: Learners delve deeper into interpreting non-linear texts like diagrams and maps. They also learn to share opinions on how these texts convey ideas. Grade 10: Learners further explore linear and non-linear texts, focusing on their applications in real-world contexts.','What is the primary advantage of using non-linear texts like charts and graphs in academic reports?','They are easier to read from start to finish.','They provide detailed narratives.','They present complex data in a concise and visual manner.','They are less common in academic settings.','C','Charts and graphs effectively display large amounts of data in a clear and organized way.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,58,'Introduction\n\nDefinition: Linear texts are those that develop smoothly and logically from one stage to the next. They are typically read from beginning to end in a sequential manner. Examples include novels, essays, and letters. Non-Linear Texts Definition: Non-linear texts do not progress in a logical or sequential manner. They can be accessed from any point and are often used to present information visually. Examples include diagrams, timelines, charts, and maps. Importance of Understanding Linear and Non-Linear Texts Understanding these text types helps students interpret information more effectively. Non- linear texts, such as diagrams and charts, are particularly useful for visual learners and can convey complex information in a concise manner. Timeline of Development Grade 7: Learners begin to identify and distinguish between linear and non-linear texts. They learn to recognize examples of each type and understand their uses. Grade 9: Learners delve deeper into interpreting non-linear texts like diagrams and maps. They also learn to share opinions on how these texts convey ideas. Grade 10: Learners further explore linear and non-linear texts, focusing on their applications in real-world contexts.','How do non-linear texts like timelines contribute to historical understanding?','By providing detailed descriptions of events.','By organizing events in chronological order, making it easier to see cause and effect.','By focusing on a single event rather than a sequence.','By ignoring the context of historical events.','B','Timelines help learners understand the progression of events and their relationships.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,59,'The city of Tokyo is known for its vibrant culture and technological advancements. It is home to numerous historical landmarks, such as the Meiji Shrine and the Tokyo Tower. Tokyo also boasts a highly efficient public transportation system, which includes trains and buses that run on precise schedules. This efficiency is partly due to the city''s emphasis on punctuality and respect for time. Additionally, Tokyo is famous for its diverse food scene, offering a wide range of traditional Japanese dishes like sushi and ramen, as well as international cuisine.','Which of the following steps would help improve the efficiency of Tokyo''s public transportation system further?','Increase the number of trains during peak hours.','Reduce the frequency of buses.','Implement a new bike-sharing system.','Increase the number of traffic lights.','A','',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,60,'The city of Tokyo is known for its vibrant culture and technological advancements. It is home to numerous historical landmarks, such as the Meiji Shrine and the Tokyo Tower. Tokyo also boasts a highly efficient public transportation system, which includes trains and buses that run on precise schedules. This efficiency is partly due to the city''s emphasis on punctuality and respect for time. Additionally, Tokyo is famous for its diverse food scene, offering a wide range of traditional Japanese dishes like sushi and ramen, as well as international cuisine.','What can be inferred about Tokyo''s cultural influence based on the passage?','Tokyo''s culture is primarily influenced by Western traditions.','Tokyo''s culture is a blend of traditional Japanese and international influences.','Tokyo''s culture is strictly traditional with no international influences.','Tokyo''s culture is not diverse.','B','Tokyo''s culture is a blend of traditional Japanese and international influences. This is supported by the mention of traditional Japanese dishes and international cuisine.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,61,'The city of Tokyo is known for its vibrant culture and technological advancements. It is home to numerous historical landmarks, such as the Meiji Shrine and the Tokyo Tower. Tokyo also boasts a highly efficient public transportation system, which includes trains and buses that run on precise schedules. This efficiency is partly due to the city''s emphasis on punctuality and respect for time. Additionally, Tokyo is famous for its diverse food scene, offering a wide range of traditional Japanese dishes like sushi and ramen, as well as international cuisine.','Which of the following statements about Tokyo is best supported by the passage?','Tokyo is known for its slow public transportation system.','Tokyo''s historical landmarks are its main tourist attractions.','Tokyo''s emphasis on punctuality contributes to its efficient public transportation.','Tokyo is famous for its lack of cultural diversity.','C','Tokyo''s emphasis on punctuality contributes to its efficient public transportation. This is supported by the passage, which mentions both punctuality and efficiency.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,62,'Imagine you are writing about a new community learning center that will be built in your neighborhood. The center will offer sports facilities, a library, and classes for adults and children. You want to inform your neighbors about this new development and encourage them to participate in the activities.','What type of text would be most effective for informing your neighbors about the new community center and encouraging them to participate?','Poem','Brochure','Short Story','Essay','B','A brochure is a practical choice for providing concise, visual information and can be easily distributed.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,63,'Imagine you are writing about a new community learning center that will be built in your neighborhood. The center will offer sports facilities, a library, and classes for adults and children. You want to inform your neighbors about this new development and encourage them to participate in the activities.','Where would you find reliable information about the community center''s facilities and programs to include in your text?','Social Media','Local Newspaper','Official City Website','Word of Mouth','C','This is likely the most reliable source for accurate and up-to-date information about the community center.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,64,'Imagine you are writing about a new community learning center that will be built in your neighborhood. The center will offer sports facilities, a library, and classes for adults and children. You want to inform your neighbors about this new development and encourage them to participate in the activities.','Why is it important to consider your target audience when choosing the type of text to write about the community center?','To ensure the text is long enough','To make sure the text is interesting to read','To tailor the content and language to the audience''s needs and understanding','To include as many details as possible','C','To tailor the content and language to the audience''s needs and understanding: This is crucial for effective communication.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,65,'Jesh is writing two different letters. The first letter is to her best friend, Diane, and it starts with "Hey Diane, what''s up?" The second letter is to her school principal, Mr. Rivero, and it begins with "Dear Mr. Rivero, I hope this letter finds you well.','Jesh needs to decide which letter to send first. If she wants to ensure that her request to the principal is processed quickly, which letter should she send first?','The letter to Diane','The letter to Mr. Rivero','Send both at the same time','Wait until she hears back from Diane','B','The letter to Mr. Rivero: Sending the letter to the principal first ensures that her request is processed quickly.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,66,'Jesh is writing two different letters. The first letter is to her best friend, Diane, and it starts with "Hey Diane, what''s up?" The second letter is to her school principal, Mr. Rivero, and it begins with "Dear Mr. Rivero, I hope this letter finds you well.','What is the main difference between the two letters Jesh is writing?','The length of the letters','The topics they discuss','The tone and formality of the letters','The color of the paper used','C','The tone and formality of the letters: This is the correct answer. The tone and formality differ between the informal letter to Diane and the formal letter to Mr. Rivero.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,67,'Jesh is writing two different letters. The first letter is to her best friend, Diane, and it starts with "Hey Diane, what''s up?" The second letter is to her school principal, Mr. Rivero, and it begins with "Dear Mr. Rivero, I hope this letter finds you well.','Why is it important for Jesh to use a formal tone in her letter to Mr. Rivero?','Because Mr. Rivero prefers informal letters','Because it shows respect and professionalism','Because it will make her request seem less important','Because it is required by school rules','B','This is the correct answer. Using a formal tone shows respect and professionalism, which is appropriate when communicating with a principal.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,68,'Dear Josh,\n\nHope you''re doing well! I just got back from a trip to the beach, and it was amazing. The weather was perfect, and I even tried surfing for the first time. It was a bit challenging, but I managed to catch a few waves.\n\nI was thinking about our plans for the summer. We should plan a trip together. Maybe we could go camping or visit a nearby city? Let me know what you think.\n\nTake care, Jesh','If you wanted to add more details about your surfing experience to the email, where would you place this information?','In the salutation','In the closing','In the body paragraphs','In the subject line','C','This is where you would add more details about your surfing experience.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,69,'Dear Josh,\n\nHope you''re doing well! I just got back from a trip to the beach, and it was amazing. The weather was perfect, and I even tried surfing for the first time. It was a bit challenging, but I managed to catch a few waves.\n\nI was thinking about our plans for the summer. We should plan a trip together. Maybe we could go camping or visit a nearby city? Let me know what you think.\n\nTake care, Jesh','What is the main purpose of the email?','To invite Josh to a party','To share summer plans with Josh','To ask Josh for advice on surfing','To tell Josh about a new job','B','To share summer plans with Josh: The email discusses plans for the summer.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,70,'Dear Josh,\n\nHope you''re doing well! I just got back from a trip to the beach, and it was amazing. The weather was perfect, and I even tried surfing for the first time. It was a bit challenging, but I managed to catch a few waves.\n\nI was thinking about our plans for the summer. We should plan a trip together. Maybe we could go camping or visit a nearby city? Let me know what you think.\n\nTake care, Jesh','Why is it important to include a closing in an email like this?','To add more details about the trip','To make the email look longer','To show politeness and end the email formally','To include a joke','C','A closing like "Take care" is polite and formal.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,71,'Angel is writing a letter to her friend who recently moved to another city. She wants to express how much she misses her and invite her to visit soon. Angel starts her letter by describing the recent events in her school and then moves on to talk about her feelings.','Angel wants to make sure her friend feels included even though she is far away. What should Angel do to achieve this in her letter?','Write about all the new friends she has made.','Share stories about their favorite memories together.','Only talk about her own life without mentioning her friend.','Tell her about all the new places she has visited.','B','This helps her friend feel connected and remembered.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,72,'Angel is writing a letter to her friend who recently moved to another city. She wants to express how much she misses her and invite her to visit soon. Angel starts her letter by describing the recent events in her school and then moves on to talk about her feelings.','What is the main purpose of Angel writing this letter?','To inform her friend about local news.','To express her feelings and invite her friend to visit.','To ask for advice on moving to another city.','To complain about her friend moving away.','B','',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,73,'Angel is writing a letter to her friend who recently moved to another city. She wants to express how much she misses her and invite her to visit soon. Angel starts her letter by describing the recent events in her school and then moves on to talk about her feelings.','Why is it important for Angel to include personal stories in her letter?','So her friend feels left out.','So her friend knows she is busy.','So her friend feels connected and remembered.','So her friend knows she is unhappy.','C','This is the most logical reason for including personal stories.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,74,'Angel is writing a letter to her friend who recently moved to another city. She wants to express how much she misses her and invite her to visit soon. Angel starts her letter by describing the recent events in her school and then moves on to talk about her feelings.','Which piece of information would best support Angel’s purpose in writing the letter?','A list of her school subjects and grades','A story about a fun memory she shared with her friend','A summary of her school’s rules and policies','A news article about her new favorite band','B','A shared memory reinforces emotional connection and strengthens the invitation.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',1,3,75,'Angel is writing a letter to her friend who recently moved to another city. She wants to express how much she misses her and invite her to visit soon. Angel starts her letter by describing the recent events in her school and then moves on to talk about her feelings.','How can Angel make her letter more meaningful for her friend?','Use formal language to show respect','Focus on daily routines to sound realistic','Share sincere feelings and a warm invitation','Include only short sentences for clarity','C','Personal feelings and an invitation clearly reflect her purpose.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,1,'Lena was always afraid of the dark. From childhood, she felt shadows moving and whispering to her. But tonight was different. She wanted to be brave. Taking a deep breath, she switched off the lights and stood in the center of her room. Her heartbeat as she attempted to get rid of the fear building up on her. For a while, there was nothing. No sound. And then, after her eyes were adjusted, Lena saw the thin light of moonlight coming from the window. The shadows no longer frightened her. She laughed at herself thinking that possibly the dark didn''t have to be as dreadful as she''d imagined.','Which of the following best describes Lena’s emotional journey?','Curious → Afraid → Panicked','Calm → Happy → Excited','Fearful → Brave → Relieved','Nervous → Angry → Safe','C','She begins with fear, chooses to be brave, and ends up relieved and amused.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,2,'Lena was always afraid of the dark. From childhood, she felt shadows moving and whispering to her. But tonight was different. She wanted to be brave. Taking a deep breath, she switched off the lights and stood in the center of her room. Her heartbeat as she attempted to get rid of the fear building up on her. For a while, there was nothing. No sound. And then, after her eyes were adjusted, Lena saw the thin light of moonlight coming from the window. The shadows no longer frightened her. She laughed at herself thinking that possibly the dark didn''t have to be as dreadful as she''d imagined.','What literary element is used when Lena thinks shadows are whispering?','Simile','Alliteration','Personification','Metaphor','C','Giving human-like traits to shadows (whispering) is personification.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,3,'Lena was always afraid of the dark. From childhood, she felt shadows moving and whispering to her. But tonight was different. She wanted to be brave. Taking a deep breath, she switched off the lights and stood in the center of her room. Her heartbeat as she attempted to get rid of the fear building up on her. For a while, there was nothing. No sound. And then, after her eyes were adjusted, Lena saw the thin light of moonlight coming from the window. The shadows no longer frightened her. She laughed at herself thinking that possibly the dark didn''t have to be as dreadful as she''d imagined.','What does Lena’s laugh at the end suggest about her mindset?','She is mocking someone','She is bored','She is starting to overcome her fear','She is completely fearless now','C','Her laughter shows she''s beginning to see her fear as exaggerated.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,4,'Lena was always afraid of the dark. From childhood, she felt shadows moving and whispering to her. But tonight was different. She wanted to be brave. Taking a deep breath, she switched off the lights and stood in the center of her room. Her heartbeat as she attempted to get rid of the fear building up on her. For a while, there was nothing. No sound. And then, after her eyes were adjusted, Lena saw the thin light of moonlight coming from the window. The shadows no longer frightened her. She laughed at herself thinking that possibly the dark didn''t have to be as dreadful as she''d imagined.','What happened to Lena when she was in the dark room?','She fell asleep in the dark.','She became brave and was not afraid anymore.','She got more scared and ran away.','She saw monsters in her room.','B','At first, Lena was afraid of the darkness, but when she stood in the room and saw the moonlight, she felt calm. She realized that the dark was not as scary as she thought.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,5,'Lena was always afraid of the dark. From childhood, she felt shadows moving and whispering to her. But tonight was different. She wanted to be brave. Taking a deep breath, she switched off the lights and stood in the center of her room. Her heartbeat as she attempted to get rid of the fear building up on her. For a while, there was nothing. No sound. And then, after her eyes were adjusted, Lena saw the thin light of moonlight coming from the window. The shadows no longer frightened her. She laughed at herself thinking that possibly the dark didn''t have to be as dreadful as she''d imagined.','Why is the moonlight important in the story?','It helped Lena see the monsters.','It made the room darker.','It helped Lena feel calm and less afraid.','It helped Lena find the door.','C','The moonlight helped Lena see that there was nothing scary in the room. It made her feel safe and helped her stop being afraid of the dark.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,6,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','What two ideas are compared through metaphor in the poem?','Trees and roads','Words and water','Language and nature','Noise and silence','C','The poem uses imagery of planting and growing to describe how words work.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,7,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','In the poem, what does “planting a tree” mean?','Talking very loudly','Thinking of something new','Reading books','Drawing a tree on paper','B','The poem says “with each thought, we plant a tree.” This means when we think, we start something good — like planting a tree that will grow.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,8,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','Which word best describes the effect of words in this poem?','Words destroy everything','Words are like magic tricks','Words help create and build ideas','Words are hard to understand','C','The poem shows that words can grow truth and knowledge, like planting seeds or trees. That means words are creative and powerful.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,9,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','Why did the poem use “seeds” as a symbol or comparison?','Because seeds can grow into something big','Because seeds are very tiny','Because seeds are easy to eat','Because seeds are weak','A','Seeds are used to show that even small ideas or words can grow into something strong and full of knowledge, just like a tree grows from a seed.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,10,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','What is the poet encouraging readers to do with words?','Use them carelessly','Stay silent','Use them to create and share truth','Write only','C','The imagery suggests words have the power to grow truth and knowledge.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,11,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','How did Sarah show that she cared for Emily?','She gave Emily money and food','She asked others to come help','She stayed quiet, nodded, and smiled','She told funny jokes','C','Sarah didn’t speak much, but her actions—listening carefully, nodding, and smiling softly—helped Emily feel supported and heard. These are ways to show care without words.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,12,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','What do Emily’s tears and words of thanks mean?','She was feeling guilty','She felt heard and thankful to Sarah','She wanted more help from others','She wished she had not talked','B','Emily cried because she was emotional, but when she said “thank you”, it showed that Sarah’s listening made her feel better. She was grateful and comforted.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,13,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','How does the story contrast speaking vs. listening?','Speaking is shown to be better','Listening is portrayed as powerful and helpful','Speaking is louder, but not helpful','Neither is shown clearly','B','The story focuses on how listening helps Emily without many words.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,14,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','What role does Sarah play in the story?','The talkative friend','The silent observer','The emotional support','The antagonist','C','Sarah supports Emily emotionally by being present and attentive.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,15,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','What actions did Sarah do that showed she was a good listener?','She gave advice quickly and walked away','She told her friend to stop talking','She listened quietly, nodded and smiled','She changed the topic of conversation','C','Sarah showed she was a good listener by being attentive and supportive. She didn’t interrupt, gave her friend space to speak, and used nonverbal cues like nodding and smiling to show she cared. These actions helped her friend feel heard and comforted, which is the mark of an active and empathetic listener.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,16,'"The Impact of Social Media Addiction"\n\nMany teenagers spend excessive hours on social media, often neglecting schoolwork and sleep. To combat this issue, schools have implemented digital wellness programs. Parents are also using screen time limits and encouraging offline activities. Additionally, app developers are creating tools that help users track and limit their social media usage. Constant social media triggers dopamine releases in the brain, creating addictive behavior patterns. This addiction leads to decreased concentration in class and lower academic performance. Because students are constantly checking their phones, they experience disrupted sleep patterns. As a result, many teenagers report feeling anxious and exhausted. While previous generations spent their free time in face-to-face interactions, today''s youth primarily connect through screens. Traditional social gatherings involve physical activities and direct communication, but modern social life often consists of likes, shares, and virtual conversations. However, both forms of interaction satisfy the basic human need for connection, albeit in different ways.','What tools are app developers creating to help with social media addiction?','Games','Cameras','Tracking and limiting features','GPS apps','C','Developers are adding tools to monitor and reduce screen time.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,17,'"The Impact of Social Media Addiction"\n\nMany teenagers spend excessive hours on social media, often neglecting schoolwork and sleep. To combat this issue, schools have implemented digital wellness programs. Parents are also using screen time limits and encouraging offline activities. Additionally, app developers are creating tools that help users track and limit their social media usage. Constant social media triggers dopamine releases in the brain, creating addictive behavior patterns. This addiction leads to decreased concentration in class and lower academic performance. Because students are constantly checking their phones, they experience disrupted sleep patterns. As a result, many teenagers report feeling anxious and exhausted. While previous generations spent their free time in face-to-face interactions, today''s youth primarily connect through screens. Traditional social gatherings involve physical activities and direct communication, but modern social life often consists of likes, shares, and virtual conversations. However, both forms of interaction satisfy the basic human need for connection, albeit in different ways.','How does social media addiction affect academic performance?','Improves it','Has no effect','Lowers it','Makes it unpredictable','C','The addiction leads to decreased concentration and poor performance.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,18,'"The Impact of Social Media Addiction"\n\nMany teenagers spend excessive hours on social media, often neglecting schoolwork and sleep. To combat this issue, schools have implemented digital wellness programs. Parents are also using screen time limits and encouraging offline activities. Additionally, app developers are creating tools that help users track and limit their social media usage. Constant social media triggers dopamine releases in the brain, creating addictive behavior patterns. This addiction leads to decreased concentration in class and lower academic performance. Because students are constantly checking their phones, they experience disrupted sleep patterns. As a result, many teenagers report feeling anxious and exhausted. While previous generations spent their free time in face-to-face interactions, today''s youth primarily connect through screens. Traditional social gatherings involve physical activities and direct communication, but modern social life often consists of likes, shares, and virtual conversations. However, both forms of interaction satisfy the basic human need for connection, albeit in different ways.','What strategies are parents using to reduce their children’s time on social media?','They tell their children to take more naps','They buy new phones for their children','They set screen time limits and suggest offline activities','They remove the internet from their homes','C','The text says that parents try to help by limiting screen time and encouraging activities that don’t use gadgets, like playing or talking with others in real life. This helps reduce the bad effects of too much phone use.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,19,'"The Impact of Social Media Addiction"\n\nMany teenagers spend excessive hours on social media, often neglecting schoolwork and sleep. To combat this issue, schools have implemented digital wellness programs. Parents are also using screen time limits and encouraging offline activities. Additionally, app developers are creating tools that help users track and limit their social media usage. Constant social media triggers dopamine releases in the brain, creating addictive behavior patterns. This addiction leads to decreased concentration in class and lower academic performance. Because students are constantly checking their phones, they experience disrupted sleep patterns. As a result, many teenagers report feeling anxious and exhausted. While previous generations spent their free time in face-to-face interactions, today''s youth primarily connect through screens. Traditional social gatherings involve physical activities and direct communication, but modern social life often consists of likes, shares, and virtual conversations. However, both forms of interaction satisfy the basic human need for connection, albeit in different ways.','How did previous generations socialize?','Through memes','Through screens','With face-to-face interactions','On social media','C','The passage contrasts screen-based interaction with traditional',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,20,'"The Impact of Social Media Addiction"\n\nMany teenagers spend excessive hours on social media, often neglecting schoolwork and sleep. To combat this issue, schools have implemented digital wellness programs. Parents are also using screen time limits and encouraging offline activities. Additionally, app developers are creating tools that help users track and limit their social media usage. Constant social media triggers dopamine releases in the brain, creating addictive behavior patterns. This addiction leads to decreased concentration in class and lower academic performance. Because students are constantly checking their phones, they experience disrupted sleep patterns. As a result, many teenagers report feeling anxious and exhausted. While previous generations spent their free time in face-to-face interactions, today''s youth primarily connect through screens. Traditional social gatherings involve physical activities and direct communication, but modern social life often consists of likes, shares, and virtual conversations. However, both forms of interaction satisfy the basic human need for connection, albeit in different ways.','What do both old and modern interactions satisfy?','The need for food','The desire to be popular','The need for connection','The love of games','C','Both forms serve the basic human need for social connection.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,21,'How to Stay Organized at Work\n\nStaying organized at work is essential for productivity and success. First, prioritize tasks by creating a to-do list. This simple yet effective method helps you focus on what needs to be done. Next, break large tasks into smaller, more manageable steps. This makes the job less overwhelming and allows you to track your progress. Additionally, maintaining a tidy workspace helps reduce distractions and improve focus. Lastly, set regular intervals to check your progress and adjust your priorities if necessary. By implementing these strategies, you can stay organized, reduce stress, and increase efficiency at work.','Why is it helpful to break big tasks into smaller steps?','Because the task will disappear','Because it makes you more stressed','Because it makes the task easier to do and check','Because it wastes your time','C','When you break big tasks into small steps, it’s easier to understand, do one step at a time, and see how much you’ve finished. This helps you feel less overwhelmed and more organized.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,22,'How to Stay Organized at Work\n\nStaying organized at work is essential for productivity and success. First, prioritize tasks by creating a to-do list. This simple yet effective method helps you focus on what needs to be done. Next, break large tasks into smaller, more manageable steps. This makes the job less overwhelming and allows you to track your progress. Additionally, maintaining a tidy workspace helps reduce distractions and improve focus. Lastly, set regular intervals to check your progress and adjust your priorities if necessary. By implementing these strategies, you can stay organized, reduce stress, and increase efficiency at work.','Which of the following is not mentioned as a strategy?','Making a to-do list','Asking for help','Tidying your workspace','Reviewing your progress','B','This is a good strategy in general but not mentioned in the passage',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,1,23,'How to Stay Organized at Work\n\nStaying organized at work is essential for productivity and success. First, prioritize tasks by creating a to-do list. This simple yet effective method helps you focus on what needs to be done. Next, break large tasks into smaller, more manageable steps. This makes the job less overwhelming and allows you to track your progress. Additionally, maintaining a tidy workspace helps reduce distractions and improve focus. Lastly, set regular intervals to check your progress and adjust your priorities if necessary. By implementing these strategies, you can stay organized, reduce stress, and increase efficiency at work.','What effect does a messy workspace have?','It encourages creativity','It helps you focus','It causes distractions','It improves communication','C','The text states a tidy space improves focus by reducing distractions',NULL,0,1),
