('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,37,NULL,'Which of the following is a statement of fact?','"Chocolate is the best flavor of ice cream."','"The Earth revolves around the Sun once every 365.25 days."','"I think students should have less homework."','"Everyone enjoys watching movies."','B','This is a verifiable fact based on astronomical observations and scientific evidence.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,38,NULL,'Which type of evidence is presented in the statement: "According to Dr. Santos, a neurologist, sleep deprivation affects memory retention"?','Anecdotal evidence','Self-evidence','Argument from authority','Empirical evidence','C','This statement relies on the expertise of a professional (Dr. Santos), which is an argument from authority.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,39,NULL,'Which of the following is an example of an opinion based on fact (fact-based opinion)?','"The movie was boring and a waste of time."','"Studies show that exercise improves mood, so everyone should work out daily."','"The sky is blue due to the scattering of sunlight by the atmosphere."','"My friend felt happier after meditating for a week."','B','This is a fact-based opinion. The first part is based on scientific studies (fact), and the second part is a recommendation or opinion derived from it.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,40,NULL,'In what way does the propaganda piece apply the “Testimonial” technique?','By citing a government official','It mentions well-known historical figures.','It features quotes from celebrities.','By referencing historical figures','C','The Testimonial technique in propaganda works by using endorsements or statements from famous or respected individuals, such as celebrities, experts, or influential figures, to persuade others to accept an idea, product, or cause.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,41,'LEARN ANYWHERE, SUCCEED EVERYWHERE! Alternative Learning System (ALS): Your Future, Your Way! What is ALS?\n\nThe Alternative Learning System (ALS) is a flexible learning program by the Department of Education for out-of-school youth, adults, and other marginalized learners who want to finish basic education outside the formal school system.'' ) "You don’t need a classroom to change your life— ( just courage and a choice."\n\n* Why Choose ALS? . - , + Flexible Schedule – Learn at your own pace. Accreditation & Certification – Earn a certificate equivalent to elementary or high school diploma. Skills-Based Curriculum – Real-life skills for real-world success. Second Chances for All – Everyone deserves to learn—no matter the age, background, or status.','Why is the use of testimonials effective in this pamphlet?','It provides academic research.','It uses confusing language to impress readers.','It shows real people succeeding through ALS.','It forces people to choose ALS.','C','Testimonials build trust and relatability. They make the message more believable by showing that someone like the reader benefited from ALS',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,42,'LEARN ANYWHERE, SUCCEED EVERYWHERE! Alternative Learning System (ALS): Your Future, Your Way! What is ALS?\n\nThe Alternative Learning System (ALS) is a flexible learning program by the Department of Education for out-of-school youth, adults, and other marginalized learners who want to finish basic education outside the formal school system.'' ) "You don’t need a classroom to change your life— ( just courage and a choice."\n\n* Why Choose ALS? . - , + Flexible Schedule – Learn at your own pace. Accreditation & Certification – Earn a certificate equivalent to elementary or high school diploma. Skills-Based Curriculum – Real-life skills for real-world success. Second Chances for All – Everyone deserves to learn—no matter the age, background, or status.','How does the pamphlet use emotional appeal?','By presenting facts and statistics.','By using cold and formal language.','By emphasizing second chances and self-worth.','By showing only logical benefits.','C','Emotional appeal targets the reader’s feelings—hope, pride, or regret—to motivate action. Words like “you deserve a second chance” touch on personal emotion.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,43,'LEARN ANYWHERE, SUCCEED EVERYWHERE! Alternative Learning System (ALS): Your Future, Your Way! What is ALS?\n\nThe Alternative Learning System (ALS) is a flexible learning program by the Department of Education for out-of-school youth, adults, and other marginalized learners who want to finish basic education outside the formal school system.'' ) "You don’t need a classroom to change your life— ( just courage and a choice."\n\n* Why Choose ALS? . - , + Flexible Schedule – Learn at your own pace. Accreditation & Certification – Earn a certificate equivalent to elementary or high school diploma. Skills-Based Curriculum – Real-life skills for real-world success. Second Chances for All – Everyone deserves to learn—no matter the age, background, or status.','Which line from the pamphlet may reflect political correctness by avoiding exclusionary language?','“Flexible Schedule – Learn at your own pace.”','“Second Chances for All – Everyone deserves to learn—no matter the age, background, or status.”','“Accreditation & Certification – Earn a certificate equivalent to elementary or high school diploma.”','“Skills-Based Curriculum – Real-life skills for real-world success.”','B','This line highlights inclusion by mentioning various identities (“age, background, or status”). It avoids exclusion and promotes political correctness by being sensitive to social diversity.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,44,'LEARN ANYWHERE, SUCCEED EVERYWHERE! Alternative Learning System (ALS): Your Future, Your Way! What is ALS?\n\nThe Alternative Learning System (ALS) is a flexible learning program by the Department of Education for out-of-school youth, adults, and other marginalized learners who want to finish basic education outside the formal school system.'' ) "You don’t need a classroom to change your life— ( just courage and a choice."\n\n* Why Choose ALS? . - , + Flexible Schedule – Learn at your own pace. Accreditation & Certification – Earn a certificate equivalent to elementary or high school diploma. Skills-Based Curriculum – Real-life skills for real-world success. Second Chances for All – Everyone deserves to learn—no matter the age, background, or status.','What propaganda technique is used in the slogan: “Alternative Learning System (ALS): Your Future, Your Way!”?','Testimonial','Transfer','Bandwagon','Slogan/Tagline Appeal','D','This is a catchy, memorable phrase meant to be easily recalled and emotionally resonant. It serves as a slogan that reflects the personalized learning offered by ALS.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,45,'LEARN ANYWHERE, SUCCEED EVERYWHERE! Alternative Learning System (ALS): Your Future, Your Way! What is ALS?\n\nThe Alternative Learning System (ALS) is a flexible learning program by the Department of Education for out-of-school youth, adults, and other marginalized learners who want to finish basic education outside the formal school system.'' ) "You don’t need a classroom to change your life— ( just courage and a choice."\n\n* Why Choose ALS? . - , + Flexible Schedule – Learn at your own pace. Accreditation & Certification – Earn a certificate equivalent to elementary or high school diploma. Skills-Based Curriculum – Real-life skills for real-world success. Second Chances for All – Everyone deserves to learn—no matter the age, background, or status.','Which statement from the ALS pamphlet best shows how it uses inclusive and politically correct language to appeal to marginalized or disadvantaged groups?','“Skills-Based Curriculum – Real-life skills for real-world success.”','“Second Chances for All – Everyone deserves to learn—no matter the age, background, or status.”','“Flexible Schedule – Learn at your own pace.”','“LEARN ANYWHERE, SUCCEED EVERYWHERE!”','B','This line deliberately emphasizes inclusivity and equal opportunity, aligning with politically correct language that promotes social justice and sensitivity to diverse learners, especially those who are typically marginalized.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,46,'The Water Cycle\n\nThe water cycle is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. It involves several key processes, including evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. Evaporation occurs when the sun heats up water in rivers, lakes, or oceans, causing it to turn into water vapor and rise into the air. Condensation happens when the water vapor cools and changes back into liquid droplets, forming clouds. When the clouds become heavy, the water falls back to the Earth as precipitation in the form of rain, snow, or hail. The water then collects in bodies of water or seeps into the ground, completing the cycle.','What is the author''s main purpose in writing the passage?','To entertain readers with stories about water','To describe the dangers of water pollution','To explain how the water cycle works and its importance','To persuade readers to visit rivers and lakes','C','The passage explains the process of the water cycle and discusses why it''s important, especially in relation to conserving water.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,47,'The Water Cycle\n\nThe water cycle is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. It involves several key processes, including evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. Evaporation occurs when the sun heats up water in rivers, lakes, or oceans, causing it to turn into water vapor and rise into the air. Condensation happens when the water vapor cools and changes back into liquid droplets, forming clouds. When the clouds become heavy, the water falls back to the Earth as precipitation in the form of rain, snow, or hail. The water then collects in bodies of water or seeps into the ground, completing the cycle.','Which group is most likely the target audience for this passage?','Scientists researching global warming','Elementary or middle school students learning science','Engineers building water systems','Farmers managing irrigation systems','B','The language used is simple and informative, making it suitable for students who are just learning about the water cycle.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,48,'The Water Cycle\n\nThe water cycle is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. It involves several key processes, including evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. Evaporation occurs when the sun heats up water in rivers, lakes, or oceans, causing it to turn into water vapor and rise into the air. Condensation happens when the water vapor cools and changes back into liquid droplets, forming clouds. When the clouds become heavy, the water falls back to the Earth as precipitation in the form of rain, snow, or hail. The water then collects in bodies of water or seeps into the ground, completing the cycle.','What is the significance of including the final paragraph in the text?','It explains how clouds are formed','It offers a summary of the water cycle','It encourages readers to take action in conserving water','It describes how precipitation works','C','The final paragraph connects the scientific explanation to real-world importance, emphasizing the need to conserve water.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,49,'The Water Cycle\n\nThe water cycle is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. It involves several key processes, including evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. Evaporation occurs when the sun heats up water in rivers, lakes, or oceans, causing it to turn into water vapor and rise into the air. Condensation happens when the water vapor cools and changes back into liquid droplets, forming clouds. When the clouds become heavy, the water falls back to the Earth as precipitation in the form of rain, snow, or hail. The water then collects in bodies of water or seeps into the ground, completing the cycle.','How does the structure of the passage help convey the author''s purpose?','It starts with facts but ends with a fictional story','It lists steps in the cycle and then reflects on their impact','It uses persuasive techniques without facts','It only focuses on underground water','B','The passage first explains the water cycle step-by-step and then discusses its significance, reinforcing the author’s purpose to inform and encourage conservation.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,2,50,'The Water Cycle\n\nThe water cycle is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. It involves several key processes, including evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. Evaporation occurs when the sun heats up water in rivers, lakes, or oceans, causing it to turn into water vapor and rise into the air. Condensation happens when the water vapor cools and changes back into liquid droplets, forming clouds. When the clouds become heavy, the water falls back to the Earth as precipitation in the form of rain, snow, or hail. The water then collects in bodies of water or seeps into the ground, completing the cycle.','Why is it important for the author to consider the target audience when writing this text?','To use complex vocabulary for better scientific accuracy','To ensure the message about water conservation is understandable','To entertain older readers with jokes','To provide specific irrigation data','B','The author tailors the explanation and vocabulary so that the intended audience (likely students) can grasp the importance of water conservation and understand how the water cycle works.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,51,'Non-Linear vs. Linear Texts Non-linear texts, such as websites or interactive books, organize information in a way that allows readers to navigate through different sections freely. These texts often use hyperlinks, menus, or indexes to connect related information. On the other hand, linear texts, like novels or essays present information in a sequential manner, where each section builds upon the previous one.','How can non-linear texts help you find information faster when doing research?','Read from beginning to end like a story.','Use links, menus, or search tools to go straight to what you need.','Read the whole text before looking for important details.','Choose linear texts only because they are easier to follow.','B','Non-linear texts -like websites or interactive books -are designed to help you move around freely and find information quickly.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,52,'Non-Linear vs. Linear Texts Non-linear texts, such as websites or interactive books, organize information in a way that allows readers to navigate through different sections freely. These texts often use hyperlinks, menus, or indexes to connect related information. On the other hand, linear texts, like novels or essays present information in a sequential manner, where each section builds upon the previous one.','What is a key feature of non-linear texts that distinguishes them from linear texts?','They are always printed on paper.','They use hyperlinks to connect related information.','They are only used for fiction.','They are always shorter than linear texts.','B','because hyperlinks are a defining feature of non-linear texts.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,53,'Non-Linear vs. Linear Texts Non-linear texts, such as websites or interactive books, organize information in a way that allows readers to navigate through different sections freely. These texts often use hyperlinks, menus, or indexes to connect related information. On the other hand, linear texts, like novels or essays present information in a sequential manner, where each section builds upon the previous one.','Why might a student prefer to use a non-linear text over a linear text for studying a complex topic?','Because non-linear texts are always easier to read.','Because they allow for a more structured approach to learning.','Because they enable students to explore topics in a flexible and interconnected way.','Because they are less engaging than linear texts.','C','Non-linear texts facilitate flexible exploration of interconnected topics.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,54,'Consider the following non-linear text about climate zones: Desert Climate: Hot and dry, found in regions like the Sahara and Mojave. Tropical Climate: Warm and humid, includes rainforests like the Amazon. Temperate Climate: Mild temperatures with seasonal changes, seen in regions like Europe and North America. Polar Climate: Extremely cold, found at the Arctic and Antarctic.','Which of the following is an example of extracting information from a non-linear text about climate zones?','Reading a linear chapter on climate zones in a textbook','Using a map to identify regions with different climates','Creating a list of characteristics for each climate zone from a non-linear source','Watching a video about climate change','C','Because it involves extracting information from a non-linear source.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,55,'Consider the following non-linear text about climate zones: Desert Climate: Hot and dry, found in regions like the Sahara and Mojave. Tropical Climate: Warm and humid, includes rainforests like the Amazon. Temperate Climate: Mild temperatures with seasonal changes, seen in regions like Europe and North America. Polar Climate: Extremely cold, found at the Arctic and Antarctic.','How can you use the information about different climate zones to critically evaluate the impact of climate change on ecosystems?','By comparing the effects of climate change on polar and desert climates','By analyzing how climate change affects only temperate climates','By considering how climate change impacts human activities in tropical climates','By evaluating the interconnected effects of climate change across all climate zones','D','Because it considers the broader impact across multiple climates.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,56,'In her essay, "The Impact of Social Media," Margie argues that excessive social media use can lead to increased stress levels. She supports her argument by quoting a study from the American Psychological Association, which states, "Exposure to idealized images of peers on social media elicits feelings of inadequacy and low self- esteem, contributing to stress." Additionally, Margie paraphrases a psychologist''s view that constant social media notifications can disrupt sleep patterns, further exacerbating stress. She also summarizes a report indicating that teenagers who limit their social media use tend to have better mental health outcomes.','How could Margie improve her argument about the impact of social media on stress levels?','By only using quotes from experts.','By incorporating more personal anecdotes.','By adding more statistical data and diverse sources.','By focusing solely on the benefits of social media.','C','Adding more statistical data and diverse sources can strengthen her argument by providing a broader perspective.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,57,'In her essay, "The Impact of Social Media," Margie argues that excessive social media use can lead to increased stress levels. She supports her argument by quoting a study from the American Psychological Association, which states, "Exposure to idealized images of peers on social media elicits feelings of inadequacy and low self- esteem, contributing to stress." Additionally, Margie paraphrases a psychologist''s view that constant social media notifications can disrupt sleep patterns, further exacerbating stress. She also summarizes a report indicating that teenagers who limit their social media use tend to have better mental health outcomes.','What type of textual evidence is Margie using in this part of her essay?','Quoting','Paraphrasing','Summarizing','Combining quoting and paraphrasing','C','Summarizing involves condensing information into a shorter form.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,58,'In her essay, "The Impact of Social Media," Margie argues that excessive social media use can lead to increased stress levels. She supports her argument by quoting a study from the American Psychological Association, which states, "Exposure to idealized images of peers on social media elicits feelings of inadequacy and low self- esteem, contributing to stress." Additionally, Margie paraphrases a psychologist''s view that constant social media notifications can disrupt sleep patterns, further exacerbating stress. She also summarizes a report indicating that teenagers who limit their social media use tend to have better mental health outcomes.','Why is it important for Margie to use a mix of quoting, paraphrasing, and summarizing in her essay?','To avoid plagiarism.','To make her essay more engaging by varying sentence structure.','To provide a balanced and comprehensive argument by presenting information in different ways.','To show off her vocabulary skills.','C','Using a mix of textual evidence types helps present information in a way that supports a comprehensive argument.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,59,'In a recent study, researchers found that reading fiction can enhance empathy in readers. The study involved two groups: one read fiction novels, and the other reads non-fiction books. After six months, participants were tested on their ability to understand others'' perspectives. The results showed that those who read fiction novels scored higher in empathy tests compared to those who read non-fiction.','What type of source would be most reliable for finding detailed information about the study mentioned in the passage?','A blog post about studying.','A news article summarizing the study.','The original research paper published in a peer-reviewed journal.','A social media post discussing the study.','C','The original research paper would offer the most detailed and reliable information.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,60,'In a recent study, researchers found that reading fiction can enhance empathy in readers. The study involved two groups: one read fiction novels, and the other reads non-fiction books. After six months, participants were tested on their ability to understand others'' perspectives. The results showed that those who read fiction novels scored higher in empathy tests compared to those who read non-fiction.','What assumption underlies the conclusion that reading fiction enhances empathy?','Reading fiction is the only way to increase empathy.','Empathy can only be developed through reading.','The act of engaging with fictional characters and their experiences helps readers understand real-life perspectives better.','Non-fiction reading has no impact on empathy.','C','This assumption aligns with the idea that fiction helps readers understand different perspectives.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,61,'When writing about environmental conservation, it''s important to organize your ideas clearly. First, introduce the topic by explaining why conservation is vital. Next, provide supporting details such as the impact of pollution and deforestation. Then, they offer solutions like recycling and reforestation. Finally, conclude by summarizing the main points and emphasizing the importance of collective action.','How can you ensure that your essay on environmental conservation is well-organized and easy to follow?','Use a chronological structure to describe events in order.','Use a cause-and-effect structure to explain how actions lead to consequences.','Use a compare-and-contrast structure to highlight differences between ideas.','Use a problem-solution structure to present issues and propose solutions.','D','Problem-solution structure is the most appropriate as it allows you to introduce the problem, provide supporting details, and offer solutions, making it easy to follow and understand.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,62,'When writing about environmental conservation, it''s important to organize your ideas clearly. First, introduce the topic by explaining why conservation is vital. Next, provide supporting details such as the impact of pollution and deforestation. Then, they offer solutions like recycling and reforestation. Finally, conclude by summarizing the main points and emphasizing the importance of collective action.','What is the primary purpose of organizing information when writing an essay?','To entertain the reader with creative ideas.','To persuade the reader with emotional appeals.','To inform the reader by presenting clear and logical arguments.','To confuse the reader with complex vocabulary.','C','Inform the reader is the primary purpose, as clear organization helps readers understand the argument.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,63,'When writing about environmental conservation, it''s important to organize your ideas clearly. First, introduce the topic by explaining why conservation is vital. Next, provide supporting details such as the impact of pollution and deforestation. Then, they offer solutions like recycling and reforestation. Finally, conclude by summarizing the main points and emphasizing the importance of collective action.','Why is it important to use transitional words when organizing significant information in an essay?','They make the text longer and more complex.','They help the reader understand the flow of ideas between paragraphs.','They are only necessary for persuasive essays.','They replace the need for clear topic sentences.','B','Help the reader understand the flow of ideas is correct, as transitional words connect ideas and improve clarity.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,64,'Understanding English Language Proficiency Levels\n\nEnglish Language Proficiency Levels are crucial for assessing the language abilities of English Learners (ELs). These levels help teachers tailor instruction to meet the diverse needs of students. The levels range from Entering (Level 1) to Bridging (Level 5), with additional levels for fully English proficient students. Understanding these levels is essential for effective teaching and learning.\n\nHere is a brief overview of the first three levels:\n\nLevel 1: Entering - Students have limited or no understanding of English and may produce isolated words or expressions.\n\nLevel 2: Emerging - Students have a limited ability to communicate in English, understanding simple directions and beginning to recognize environmental print.\n\nLevel 3: Developing - Students can communicate more effectively, with increasing vocabulary and the ability to follow complex verbal directions.','What is the main purpose of understanding English Language Proficiency Levels for teachers of English Learners?','To help teachers adjust their instruction based on students'' language abilities','To group students for extracurricular activities','To assign students to grade levels','To determine students'' reading levels only','A','Helps teachers tailor instruction to meet the diverse needs of students',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,65,'Understanding English Language Proficiency Levels\n\nEnglish Language Proficiency Levels are crucial for assessing the language abilities of English Learners (ELs). These levels help teachers tailor instruction to meet the diverse needs of students. The levels range from Entering (Level 1) to Bridging (Level 5), with additional levels for fully English proficient students. Understanding these levels is essential for effective teaching and learning.\n\nHere is a brief overview of the first three levels:\n\nLevel 1: Entering - Students have limited or no understanding of English and may produce isolated words or expressions.\n\nLevel 2: Emerging - Students have a limited ability to communicate in English, understanding simple directions and beginning to recognize environmental print.\n\nLevel 3: Developing - Students can communicate more effectively, with increasing vocabulary and the ability to follow complex verbal directions.','Why is it important for teachers to understand the distinction between social language and academic language when teaching English Learners?','So they can focus solely on social language skills.','To ensure students can communicate effectively in both social and academic settings.','Because academic language is easier to learn than social language.','To prioritize teaching only academic vocabulary.','B','Understanding both types of language helps teachers support students in all contexts, ensuring effective communication in both social and academic settings. WIDA. (2020). English language development standards framework, 2020 edition: Kindergarten–grade 12. Board of Regents of the University of Wisconsin System. https://wida.wisc.edu/teach/standards/eld',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,66,'Leonie is writing a letter to her friend, Sarah, who lives in another city. She wants to tell Sarah about her recent trip to the beach. Here is a draft of her letter:\n\nDear Marie,\n\nHi! How are you? I hope you''re doing well. I just got back from the most amazing trip to the beach! The weather was perfect, and I spent hours swimming and sunbathing. I even tried surfing for the first time, which was super fun but also really hard.\n\nAnyway, I wish you were there because it would have been even more fun with you. Let''s plan a trip together soon!\n\nTake care, Leonie','What type of information does Leonie''s letter primarily contain?','Formal instructions','Personal experiences and feelings','Historical facts','Scientific data','B','The letter is about Leonie''s personal experiences and feelings from her trip.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,67,'Leonie is writing a letter to her friend, Sarah, who lives in another city. She wants to tell Sarah about her recent trip to the beach. Here is a draft of her letter:\n\nDear Marie,\n\nHi! How are you? I hope you''re doing well. I just got back from the most amazing trip to the beach! The weather was perfect, and I spent hours swimming and sunbathing. I even tried surfing for the first time, which was super fun but also really hard.\n\nAnyway, I wish you were there because it would have been even more fun with you. Let''s plan a trip together soon!\n\nTake care, Leonie','Why is Leonie''s letter considered informal?','Because it lacks a formal closing.','Because it discusses personal experiences and uses casual language.','Because it is not typed.','Because it does not have a specific date.','B','The letter is informal because it discusses personal experiences and uses casual language.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,68,'Ms. Guarin, a junior high school teacher, is preparing her students for a formal letter-writing exercise. She explains that formal letters are typically used in professional settings, such as applying for jobs or writing to government officials. Informal letters, on the other hand, are used among friends and family. Ms. Guarin provides examples of both types of letters to illustrate their differences. She notes that formal letters usually include formal greetings, proper titles, and a more structured format, while informal letters are more casual and conversational.','Where would Ms. Guarin likely find examples of formal and informal letters to use in her lesson?','Social media platforms','Textbooks or educational websites','Local newspapers','Personal letters from friends','B','because textbooks and educational websites often provide structured examples of formal and informal letters.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,69,'Ms. Guarin, a junior high school teacher, is preparing her students for a formal letter-writing exercise. She explains that formal letters are typically used in professional settings, such as applying for jobs or writing to government officials. Informal letters, on the other hand, are used among friends and family. Ms. Guarin provides examples of both types of letters to illustrate their differences. She notes that formal letters usually include formal greetings, proper titles, and a more structured format, while informal letters are more casual and conversational.','Why is it important for students to understand the difference between formal and informal correspondence?','So they can communicate more effectively in personal relationships.','To avoid using slang in formal situations.','To ensure they use proper grammar and spelling in all communications.','To adapt their communication style appropriately to different contexts and audiences.','D','This understanding the difference allows students to communicate effectively in various contexts, such as professional or academic settings, by adapting their style to the audience and purpose.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,70,'Dear Team,\n\nI hope this message finds you well. As you know, our office has been experiencing some challenges with the current work schedule. To better align with our client needs and improve productivity, we will be adjusting our office hours starting next Monday. The new hours will be from 8:00 AM to 4:00 PM. Please ensure that you are punctual and ready to start work at the new time. If you have any concerns or need further clarification, please do not hesitate to reach out to me directly.\n\nThank you for your cooperation and understanding.\n\nBest regards, Josh Urbano','What is the primary reason for adjusting the office hours, according to the email?','To accommodate employee preferences.','To align with client needs and improve productivity.','To reduce operational costs.','To comply with new government regulations.','B','The email explicitly states that the change is to align with client needs and improve productivity.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,71,'Dear Team,\n\nI hope this message finds you well. As you know, our office has been experiencing some challenges with the current work schedule. To better align with our client needs and improve productivity, we will be adjusting our office hours starting next Monday. The new hours will be from 8:00 AM to 4:00 PM. Please ensure that you are punctual and ready to start work at the new time. If you have any concerns or need further clarification, please do not hesitate to reach out to me directly.\n\nThank you for your cooperation and understanding.\n\nBest regards, Josh Urbano','What tone does the manager use in the email?','Strict and commanding.','Friendly and casual.','Polite and professional.','Angry and dismissive.','C','The tone is polite and professional, as indicated by the language used.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,72,'When writing a formal email to a teacher or employer, it''s important to use proper formatting and structure. This includes starting with a formal greeting, followed by a clear introduction, a concise body, and a polite closing. In contrast, informal emails to friends might be more casual and lack these formal elements.','Where would you find reliable information about the proper structure of formal emails?','Social media posts.','Academic journals.','Online tutorials or educational websites.','Text messages from friends.','C','designed to provide clear guidelines on formal email structures.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,73,'When writing a formal email to a teacher or employer, it''s important to use proper formatting and structure. This includes starting with a formal greeting, followed by a clear introduction, a concise body, and a polite closing. In contrast, informal emails to friends might be more casual and lack these formal elements.','Why is it important to use a formal structure in emails to teachers or employers?','It shows that you are very busy and can''t spend time on details.','It helps ensure that your message is clear and professional.','It makes the email longer and more complicated.','It is only necessary for very important messages.','B','A formal structure indeed helps convey professionalism and clarity.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,74,'Ms. Mary, a junior high school student, is writing a formal email to her school principal, Mr. Jasock, to request permission to organize a school event. Here is a draft of her email:\n\nDear Mr. Jasock,\n\nI hope this email finds you well. My classmates and I are interested in organizing a school event to celebrate the end of the semester. The event would include a talent show, a bake sale, and a small concert. We believe this event would be a great way to bring the school community together and provide an opportunity for students to showcase their talents.\n\nWe have planned the event for the last Friday of the semester. We would need access to the school auditorium and some tables for the bake sale. We are confident that this event will be a success and would be grateful if you could approve our request.\n\nThank you for considering our proposal Sincerely, Ms. Mary','What is the primary purpose of including the phrase "I hope this email finds you well" in formal correspondence?','To express personal concern for the recipient''s health.','To establish a formal tone.','To soften the tone of the message.','To introduce the main topic of the email.','C','Because it helps to soften the tone by adding a polite and courteous element.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',2,3,75,'Ms. Mary, a junior high school student, is writing a formal email to her school principal, Mr. Jasock, to request permission to organize a school event. Here is a draft of her email:\n\nDear Mr. Jasock,\n\nI hope this email finds you well. My classmates and I are interested in organizing a school event to celebrate the end of the semester. The event would include a talent show, a bake sale, and a small concert. We believe this event would be a great way to bring the school community together and provide an opportunity for students to showcase their talents.\n\nWe have planned the event for the last Friday of the semester. We would need access to the school auditorium and some tables for the bake sale. We are confident that this event will be a success and would be grateful if you could approve our request.\n\nThank you for considering our proposal Sincerely, Ms. Mary','What possible challenge might Ms. Mary encounter if she does not receive a response from Mr. Jasock before the scheduled date of the school event?','The event might not receive the necessary approval.','The school auditorium could already be reserved for another activity.','The event may proceed without needing any permission.','The principal’s silence would mean automatic approval.','A','Proceeding without approval could lead to legal or administrative issues.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,1,'Lena was always afraid of the dark. From childhood, she felt shadows moving and whispering to her. But tonight was different. She wanted to be brave. Taking a deep breath, she switched off the lights and stood in the center of her room. Her heartbeat as she attempted to get rid of the fear building up on her. For a while, there was nothing. No sound. And then, after her eyes were adjusted, Lena saw the thin light of moonlight coming from the window. The shadows no longer frightened her. She laughed at herself thinking that possibly the dark didn''t have to be as dreadful as she''d imagined.','How does Lena''s transformation reflect a universal theme?','It shows that everyone likes the night','It illustrates how people enjoy challenges','It reflects the theme of confronting fears and personal growth','It proves fear is an illusion','C','Lena feared the dark but chose to face it. This shows how people can grow by facing their fears.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,2,'Lena was always afraid of the dark. From childhood, she felt shadows moving and whispering to her. But tonight was different. She wanted to be brave. Taking a deep breath, she switched off the lights and stood in the center of her room. Her heartbeat as she attempted to get rid of the fear building up on her. For a while, there was nothing. No sound. And then, after her eyes were adjusted, Lena saw the thin light of moonlight coming from the window. The shadows no longer frightened her. She laughed at herself thinking that possibly the dark didn''t have to be as dreadful as she''d imagined.','If Lena hadn’t turned off the lights, what might the theme have been instead?','Comfort in technology','Avoiding responsibilities','Living with unresolved fear','Curiosity and adventure','C','If Lena didn’t turn off the lights, she would still be afraid. The story would then show fear that was never overcome.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,3,'Lena was always afraid of the dark. From childhood, she felt shadows moving and whispering to her. But tonight was different. She wanted to be brave. Taking a deep breath, she switched off the lights and stood in the center of her room. Her heartbeat as she attempted to get rid of the fear building up on her. For a while, there was nothing. No sound. And then, after her eyes were adjusted, Lena saw the thin light of moonlight coming from the window. The shadows no longer frightened her. She laughed at herself thinking that possibly the dark didn''t have to be as dreadful as she''d imagined.','Which symbol best represents Lena''s emotional shift?','Her laughter','The room','The silence','The window','A','Her laughter shows how her emotions changed—from fear to realizing she was no longer afraid.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,4,'Lena was always afraid of the dark. From childhood, she felt shadows moving and whispering to her. But tonight was different. She wanted to be brave. Taking a deep breath, she switched off the lights and stood in the center of her room. Her heartbeat as she attempted to get rid of the fear building up on her. For a while, there was nothing. No sound. And then, after her eyes were adjusted, Lena saw the thin light of moonlight coming from the window. The shadows no longer frightened her. She laughed at herself thinking that possibly the dark didn''t have to be as dreadful as she''d imagined.','How does the story use contrast to build meaning?','Between night and day','Between fear and courage','Between room size and shadow size','Between moonlight and firelight','B','The story contrasts Lena’s fear at the beginning with her bravery at the end.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,5,'Lena was always afraid of the dark. From childhood, she felt shadows moving and whispering to her. But tonight was different. She wanted to be brave. Taking a deep breath, she switched off the lights and stood in the center of her room. Her heartbeat as she attempted to get rid of the fear building up on her. For a while, there was nothing. No sound. And then, after her eyes were adjusted, Lena saw the thin light of moonlight coming from the window. The shadows no longer frightened her. She laughed at herself thinking that possibly the dark didn''t have to be as dreadful as she''d imagined.','What might the dark symbolize beyond literal darkness?','A time to sleep','A dangerous place','The unknown or internal fears','A cool environment','C','Darkness here is more than just the absence of light—it represents fear or the unknown.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,6,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','What is the underlying message of the poem?','Trees are essential for life','Words are tools of destruction','Words can shape reality and ideas','Nature is better than technology','C','Darkness here is more than just the absence of light—it represents fear or the unknown.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,7,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','If you extended the metaphor, what might a “forest” of trees represent?','A crowded room','A library or collective knowledge','A noisy debate','An outdoor garden','B','If one word is like a tree, then many words (a forest) could be a library full of knowledge.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,8,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','How could this poem be used in a classroom setting?','To practice planting real trees','To teach gardening vocabulary','To introduce metaphor and abstract thinking','To explain botany','C','The poem uses nature (trees, seeds) as metaphors to explain ideas. It''s a good way to teach creative thinking.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,9,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','How would removing nature imagery affect the poem?','It would become more complex','It would lose emotional power and clarity','It would become scientific','It would sound like a joke','B','Without nature images, the poem would sound less powerful and would be harder to understand emotionally.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,10,'The Power of Words\n\nWords can make the world we see, With each thought, we plant a tree. In each sentence, truth can grow, A seed of knowledge we can sow.','What philosophical idea is explored through the poem?','Silence is stronger than words','Knowledge is instinctive','Words have the power to build or shape reality','Only action matters, not speech','C','The whole poem is about how words can create change, ideas, and truth— like planting seeds.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,11,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','What theme does the story express most strongly?','Time heals all wounds','The power of silence and empathy','Always speak your mind','Friends should always give advice','B','Sarah didn’t speak much, but by listening, she showed empathy and helped her friend feel better.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,12,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','What lesson can we learn from what Sarah did in the story?','Talk a lot so others will listen to you','Expensive gifts are the best way to comfort someone','Listening carefully can help someone feel better','Do not share your problems with friends','C','Sarah didn’t give advice—she just listened, and that was enough to help Emily.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,13,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','What important life lessons can apply from Sarah’s actions in the story?','We should always try to win when people disagree with us','Being silent shows we have no strength','Listening with empathy can comfort and support others','Avoiding people with problems keeps stress free','C','',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,14,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','What literary device is used in “the greatest gift one could offer wasn''t words”?','Simile','Personification','Irony','Theme','C','It’s ironic that “not speaking” was the most helpful act. Usually, we think talking helps, but here silence did.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,15,'"The Gift of Listening"\n\nSarah had always been the quiet one in the group. She preferred listening to talking. One day, her friend Emily came to her, looking distressed. Emily started talking about her troubles at home, her struggles with school, and how overwhelmed she felt. Sarah didn’t interrupt. She just listened, nodding occasionally, and offered a soft smile when Emily’s voice faltered. After a while, Emily seemed to calm down. “Thank you, Sarah,” she said, wiping away a tear. “You didn’t even say much, but just listening helped me more than you know.” Sarah smiled, feeling warmth in her heart. Sometimes, the greatest gift one could offer wasn’t words, but simply the act of listening and being present.','If the story were told from Emily’s point of view, how might it change?','It would be more focused on silence','We would see how she interpreted Sarah''s presence emotionally','It would have more action','It would be a story about a party','B','From Emily’s view, we would understand how Sarah’s quiet support helped her emotionally.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,16,'"The Impact of Social Media Addiction"\n\nMany teenagers spend excessive hours on social media, often neglecting schoolwork and sleep. To combat this issue, schools have implemented digital wellness programs. Parents are also using screen time limits and encouraging offline activities. Additionally, app developers are creating tools that help users track and limit their social media usage. Constant social media triggers dopamine releases in the brain, creating addictive behavior patterns. This addiction leads to decreased concentration in class and lower academic performance. Because students are constantly checking their phones, they experience disrupted sleep patterns. As a result, many teenagers report feeling anxious and exhausted. While previous generations spent their free time in face-to-face interactions, today''s youth primarily connect through screens. Traditional social gatherings involve physical activities and direct communication, but modern social life often consists of likes, shares, and virtual conversations. However, both forms of interaction satisfy the basic human need for connection, albeit in different ways.','How does dopamine help explain why many teenagers get addicted to social media?','It makes them sleepy and tired','It helps them remember things better','It makes them feel good, so they want to keep using it.','It stops them from enjoying real life','C','Dopamine gives a "feel-good" feeling, which makes teens return to social media again and again.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,17,'"The Impact of Social Media Addiction"\n\nMany teenagers spend excessive hours on social media, often neglecting schoolwork and sleep. To combat this issue, schools have implemented digital wellness programs. Parents are also using screen time limits and encouraging offline activities. Additionally, app developers are creating tools that help users track and limit their social media usage. Constant social media triggers dopamine releases in the brain, creating addictive behavior patterns. This addiction leads to decreased concentration in class and lower academic performance. Because students are constantly checking their phones, they experience disrupted sleep patterns. As a result, many teenagers report feeling anxious and exhausted. While previous generations spent their free time in face-to-face interactions, today''s youth primarily connect through screens. Traditional social gatherings involve physical activities and direct communication, but modern social life often consists of likes, shares, and virtual conversations. However, both forms of interaction satisfy the basic human need for connection, albeit in different ways.','How has the way teenagers connect changed over generations?','They now only talk in person','They don’t connect at all','They rely more on digital and virtual communication','They avoid friendships','C','Today’s teens mostly connect through online platforms, unlike face-to-face communication before.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,18,'"The Impact of Social Media Addiction"\n\nMany teenagers spend excessive hours on social media, often neglecting schoolwork and sleep. To combat this issue, schools have implemented digital wellness programs. Parents are also using screen time limits and encouraging offline activities. Additionally, app developers are creating tools that help users track and limit their social media usage. Constant social media triggers dopamine releases in the brain, creating addictive behavior patterns. This addiction leads to decreased concentration in class and lower academic performance. Because students are constantly checking their phones, they experience disrupted sleep patterns. As a result, many teenagers report feeling anxious and exhausted. While previous generations spent their free time in face-to-face interactions, today''s youth primarily connect through screens. Traditional social gatherings involve physical activities and direct communication, but modern social life often consists of likes, shares, and virtual conversations. However, both forms of interaction satisfy the basic human need for connection, albeit in different ways.','Which of the following best summarizes the overall problem in the text?','Phones are too expensive','Teenagers are lazy','Social media is causing academic and health problems','Technology is dangerous','C','The paragraph focuses on how too much social media use harms sleep, focus, and school performance.',NULL,0,1),
('JHS','LS1-EN',3,1,19,'"The Impact of Social Media Addiction"\n\nMany teenagers spend excessive hours on social media, often neglecting schoolwork and sleep. To combat this issue, schools have implemented digital wellness programs. Parents are also using screen time limits and encouraging offline activities. Additionally, app developers are creating tools that help users track and limit their social media usage. Constant social media triggers dopamine releases in the brain, creating addictive behavior patterns. This addiction leads to decreased concentration in class and lower academic performance. Because students are constantly checking their phones, they experience disrupted sleep patterns. As a result, many teenagers report feeling anxious and exhausted. While previous generations spent their free time in face-to-face interactions, today''s youth primarily connect through screens. Traditional social gatherings involve physical activities and direct communication, but modern social life often consists of likes, shares, and virtual conversations. However, both forms of interaction satisfy the basic human need for connection, albeit in different ways.','What is one big difference between how teenagers interact now and how people interacted in the past?','Teenagers today prefer talking in person','People in the past used cell phones','Teenagers today mostly connect through screens, while before, people met face-to- face','There is no difference','C','The text explains how interactions have changed—from real-life gatherings to online chats.',NULL,0,1),
