('JHS','LS3',3,2,48,NULL,'You are presented with two bar graphs representing the monthly sales figures for two different brands of smartphones over six months. Graph A is based on primary data collected from actual sales records. Graph B is based on secondary data from a market research article. Graph B uses a truncated y-axis starting at 50 instead of 0, making differences appear larger than they are. Analyze both graphs and determine whether either graph might be misleading. Support your answer by referencing the type of data (primary or secondary) and the graph presentation.','Graph B, on the other hand, is based on secondary data, which may have already been processed, summarized, or interpreted. Furthermore, the use of a truncated y-axis starting at 50 instead of 0 is a visual technique that exaggerates the differences between values. For instance, if one brand sold 55 units and another sold 60, the graph may make the difference appear much more significant than it actually is.','Graph A, based on primary data, shows actual sales figures with a full y-axis starting from zero. This is typically more reliable and transparent, as the data source is direct and the graph scale is not distorted. It provides an accurate visual representation of the differences in monthly sales between the two smartphone brands.','Both A and B are not misleading graphs.','Graph A and B are both misleading.','A','',NULL,0,0),
